      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research a sparse-obstacle combinatorial algorithm. Deduplicate blocked (row, column) coordinates, sort by row+column, and compute each blocker’s first-hit path count by subtracting contributions from coordinatewise predecessor blockers, using binomial coefficients modulo 1,000,000,007. Subtract the blockers’ contributions from C(2N−2,N−1) for the answer. Handle blocked start/end explicitly; use 64-bit modular products and verify coordinate orientation and equal-diagonal ordering. With N<200,000, factorials through 2N−2 fit below the modulus. This costs O(M²), so measure a work-based crossover against dense DP rather than using it when M is large. Preserve correctness for every valid input.\",\"B\":\"Research a memory-efficient dense-DP baseline. Replace the N×N blocked matrix and two rolling rows plus row-copy with one in-place O(N) DP row; while scanning each row, reset blocked cells to zero and otherwise add the previous-row value to the current-row left value modulo the modulus. Store/deduplicate blocked coordinates sparsely by row so membership checks do not require N² storage. This remains O(N²) time, so measure it against the sparse combinatorial approach, including allocation and input costs. Preserve the baseline’s (row, column) interpretation and blocked-start/end behavior.\",\"C\":\"Research parallelization of the dense recurrence for the 4-core ARM64 target: prototype a tiled wavefront schedule, since each tile depends on its top and left neighbors and tiles on the same wavefront can run concurrently. Keep blocked-cell storage sparse and avoid a full N×N bitmap; retain an efficient serial path for sizes where scheduling overhead dominates. Measure correctness and end-to-end runtime on the unchanged runner, and compare against both the in-place dense and sparse combinatorial candidates. Do not assume parallel speedup or change the compiler/toolchain.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-validated candidate programs from A, B, and C. Benchmark the integrated executable end-to-end against the baseline and the individual candidates; gains may not add, so retain an algorithm or work-estimate-based dispatcher only if the integrated result itself measures better. Check all valid-input edge cases (including no obstacles, duplicate blocked coordinates, and blocked start/end), modular arithmetic, and required output. Do not specialize to test values or alter tests, oracle, compiler/toolchain, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied source is serial O(N²), allocates an N×N blocked bitmap, and copies a rolling row each iteration. The run’s baseline is valid on 4 cases, with mean time 0.0795496525 s and score 0.5; no per-case N or M measurements are supplied, so the best crossover or parallel granularity cannot be inferred. The objectives therefore test complementary improvements: sparse combinatorial counting, lower-memory dense DP, and a measured parallel dense kernel, with integration judged empirically rather than assuming additive gains.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"aebdf1abd6968981f722e7f6dfcd3718492f08946bb742e0b201bbf9d0be89f5\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research a sparse-obstacle combinatorial algorithm. Deduplicate blocked (row, column) coordinates, sort by row+column, and compute each blocker’s first-hit path count by subtracting contributions from coordinatewise predecessor blockers, using binomial coefficients modulo 1,000,000,007. Subtract the blockers’ contributions from C(2N−2,N−1) for the answer. Handle blocked start/end explicitly; use 64-bit modular products and verify coordinate orientation and equal-diagonal ordering. With N<200,000, factorials through 2N−2 fit below the modulus. This costs O(M²), so measure a work-based crossover against dense DP rather than using it when M is large. Preserve correctness for every valid input.\",\"B\":\"Research a memory-efficient dense-DP baseline. Replace the N×N blocked matrix and two rolling rows plus row-copy with one in-place O(N) DP row; while scanning each row, reset blocked cells to zero and otherwise add the previous-row value to the current-row left value modulo the modulus. Store/deduplicate blocked coordinates sparsely by row so membership checks do not require N² storage. This remains O(N²) time, so measure it against the sparse combinatorial approach, including allocation and input costs. Preserve the baseline’s (row, column) interpretation and blocked-start/end behavior.\",\"C\":\"Research parallelization of the dense recurrence for the 4-core ARM64 target: prototype a tiled wavefront schedule, since each tile depends on its top and left neighbors and tiles on the same wavefront can run concurrently. Keep blocked-cell storage sparse and avoid a full N×N bitmap; retain an efficient serial path for sizes where scheduling overhead dominates. Measure correctness and end-to-end runtime on the unchanged runner, and compare against both the in-place dense and sparse combinatorial candidates. Do not assume parallel speedup or change the compiler/toolchain.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-validated candidate programs from A, B, and C. Benchmark the integrated executable end-to-end against the baseline and the individual candidates; gains may not add, so retain an algorithm or work-estimate-based dispatcher only if the integrated result itself measures better. Check all valid-input edge cases (including no obstacles, duplicate blocked coordinates, and blocked start/end), modular arithmetic, and required output. Do not specialize to test values or alter tests, oracle, compiler/toolchain, budgets, target, or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The supplied source is serial O(N²), allocates an N×N blocked bitmap, and copies a rolling row each iteration. The run’s baseline is valid on 4 cases, with mean time 0.0795496525 s and score 0.5; no per-case N or M measurements are supplied, so the best crossover or parallel granularity cannot be inferred. The objectives therefore test complementary improvements: sparse combinatorial counting, lower-memory dense DP, and a measured parallel dense kernel, with integration judged empirically rather than assuming additive gains.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"fbcb1220257342428c9e48a3035dfc2b\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"aebdf1abd6968981f722e7f6dfcd3718492f08946bb742e0b201bbf9d0be89f5\",\"session_id\":\"4b9f09d381a1468688f991832d964d17\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"b13b7c07368c2af8770280ae323e6791125f4934ab1f00cfd3539689ce93ed2d\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":9959,\"output_tokens\":7402,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":6780}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":17361,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
